@startuml proto-sr04-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-sr04-frame.puml - (b) the HC-SR04 exchange, the four measured
' conversions, and the image.
'
' HC-SR04 is the only act in the family whose OUTPUT IS A QUANTITY rather than a
' waveform. So the "frame" is a measurement envelope - trigger, recovery, echo
' pulse - and the payload is a pulse whose WIDTH is the distance.
'
' STATUS: GREEN. An earlier revision of this figure drew the act as red with two
' targets and the second failing; the supersession is recorded at the foot rather
' than quietly overwritten.
' ===========================================================================

title HC-SR04 - the exchange, the four conversions, and the image\n<font size=12>STATUS: GREEN - 199 / 999 / 343 / 1375 mm, every one exact</font>

left to right direction
hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<host>> {
  BackgroundColor #DCE6F5
  BorderColor #5577AA
}
skinparam rectangle<<dev>> {
  BackgroundColor #D6F5D6
  BorderColor #3A7A3A
}
skinparam rectangle<<ok>> {
  BackgroundColor #D6F5D6
  BorderColor #3A7A3A
}
skinparam rectangle<<gate>> {
  BackgroundColor #FFF4D6
  BorderColor #C8B800
}

rectangle "TRIGGER\n<b>host</b>, pin 6\nSR_TRIG = 0x40 (the PIN BIT)\n\n<b>4 * SR_TRIG_LEN + 5</b>\n= 4 * 149 + 5\n= <b>601 clocks</b>\n= <b>10.0167 us</b>\n\nthe device asks for\n10 us MINIMUM" as T <<host>>
rectangle "RECOVERY\n<b>the sensor settles</b>\n\nSR_RECOV_LEN = 117\nthe absence of this wait\nwas a real defect once:\na program that measures\nimmediately is reading\nthe recovery" as R <<dev>>
rectangle "8 x 40 kHz BURSTS\n<b>the device</b>\n\nthe device emits a chirp\nand then holds ECHO\nhigh for the round trip\n\n<b>the firmware does not\nlook at these</b> - it cannot\nresynchronise to a burst\nit did not send" as B <<dev>>
rectangle "ECHO\n<b>device</b>, pin 5, RELEASED\nand READ BACK\n\nSR_ECHO = 0x20\n\n<b>HIGH FOR THE ROUND TRIP</b>\n\n<b>THE ANSWER IS THE WIDTH,\nnot a level.</b>" as E <<dev>>
rectangle "MEASURE + CONVERT\n\n<b>the 1 us counter as a\nDIFFERENCE, not the flag</b>\n\nmm = us * 11/64\n<b>EXACT for 11/64</b>" as C <<host>>
rectangle "BANK\n\n4 runs banked\nall four correct" as K <<ok>>

T -[#3A7]-> R
R -[#3A7]-> B
B -[#3A7]-> E
E -[#3A7]-> C
C -[#3A7]-> K
K -[#3A7,dashed]-> T : the next run

note top of T
  <b>SR_TRIG_LEN IS NOT SR_TRIG, and the two are different
  KINDS of thing.</b>
  ...
  | constant | value | what it is |
  | <b>SR_TRIG</b> | 0x40 | the PIN BIT - which pad drives the trigger |
  | <b>SR_TRIG_LEN</b> | 149 | the DELAY COUNT - how long it is held |
  | SR_ECHO | 0x20 | the pin bit, released and read back |
  | SR_RECOV_LEN | 117 | the sensor's recovery wait |
  ...
  Both SR_TRIG and SR_TRIG_LEN exist in peasm's CONSTS, which is exactly why
  naming the wrong one is easy and why a figure that printed
  <b>4 * SR_TRIG + 4 = 600 clocks</b> was wrong three ways at once: the +4
  should be +5, the count is 601 not 600, and the constant has the wrong name.
  ...
  <b>The testbench pins 601 as an EQUALITY,</b> so a 600-clock derivation clears
  every datasheet window in print and still fails the act's own claim.
end note

note right of E
  <b>WHY THE ECHO IS CAPTURED AND NOT SAMPLED,</b> which is
  what makes this act safe at 4 m.

  A receiver that samples the level and decides "there is
  something there" has to poll, and a poll loop that is
  long relative to the distance reading adds its own
  uncertainty to every sample. A receiver that measures
  the *width* does not care when it looked: the two
  edges define the interval, and the interval is the
  answer.
  ...
  The same reason the counter is RESET at the rising edge
  and READ at the falling edge rather than subtracted:
  no 16-bit subtract, and this machine has no carry flag,
  so a subtract is a four-case analysis on bit 7 and a
  compare is the same analysis again. **One reset removes
  both** - the design [protocol-freqmeter] arrived at
  independently, and for the same reason.
end note

note bottom of C
  <b>THE IDENTITY, and why it removes the multiply the plan
  budgeted for.</b>
  ...
    us = 64q + r
    us * 11 / 64  =  11q + 11r/64  =  11q + floor(11r/64)
  ...
  <b>EXACTLY, because 11q is an integer.</b> So the conversion is a multiply by
  eleven of a ten-bit number, a multiply by eleven of a six-bit number, and one
  add. No division, no approximation.
  ...
  <b>A MULTIPLY BY ELEVEN IS FOUR DOUBLINGS AND TWO ADDS:</b>
  x -> 2x -> 4x -> 5x -> 10x -> 11x. No loop, no counter, no second
  temporary - the multiplicand stays in Q throughout.
  ...
  <b>A DOUBLING IS THE CHEAPEST 16-BIT OPERATION THIS ISA HAS,</b> and unlike any
  other 16-bit add it needs no both-zero guard. The carry out of lo+lo is bit 7 of
  the ORIGINAL low byte, so one AND with 0x80 and one JNZ is the whole test; and
  "the result is zero" means "nothing carried", because doubling zero cannot carry
  and is the only way to reach zero. A general add of a variable IS different:
  0 + 0 = 0 is not a carry, so the addends must be re-read. <b>That asymmetry is
  the reason the conversion is mostly doublings.</b>
  ...
  <b>No 16-bit overflow is possible anywhere:</b> the largest value is
  11 * (23000 >> 6) = 3949, the intermediates are 8q = 2872 and 10q = 3590, and
  the second term is 11 * 63 = 693. Recorded rather than assumed, because an
  unnoticed overflow in the high byte is invisible from the outside - the answer
  would still be a plausible millimetre figure.
end note

rectangle "THE FOUR MEASURED CONVERSIONS - and the one where truncation is a no-op" as HDR <<gate>>

rectangle "run 0\n\necho <b>1 160 us</b>\n\n1 160 x 11/64\n= 199.375\n-> <b>199 mm</b>\n\n<font size=10>product exact,\n199 is its\ntruncation</font>" as P0 <<ok>>
rectangle "run 1\n\necho <b>5 816 us</b>\n\n5 816 x 11/64\n= 999.4375\n-> <b>999 mm</b>\n\n<font size=10>exercises the ten-bit\npath AND the high\nbyte</font>" as P1 <<ok>>
rectangle "run 2\n\necho <b>2 000 us</b>\n\n2 000 x 11/64\n= 343.75\n-> <b>343 mm</b>\n\n<font size=10>product exact,\n343 is its\ntruncation</font>" as P2 <<ok>>
rectangle "run 3\n\necho <b>8 000 us</b>\n\n8 000 x 11/64\n= <b>1375 exactly</b>\n-> <b>1375 mm</b>\n\n<font size=10>8000 = 64 x 125, so\nr = 0 and floor() is\na NO-OP</font>" as P3 <<ok>>

HDR -[#3A7]-> P0
HDR -[#3A7]-> P1
HDR -[#3A7]-> P2
HDR -[#3A7]-> P3

note bottom of P3
  <b>"EXACT" MEANS TWO THINGS IN THIS ACT, AND RUN 3 IS THE
  CASE THAT TELLS THEM APART.</b>
  ...
  <b>1. THE IDENTITY IS EXACT.</b> us*11/64 = 11q + floor(11r/64) introduces no
  rounding error at all. A statement about the ALGORITHM, and the one the firmware's
  own header makes.
  ...
  <b>2. THE ARITHMETIC IS EXACT AND THE ANSWER IS TRUNCATED.</b> 199.375 is exact;
  199 is floor of it. So "PASS, an equality" is exact only AGAINST A TRUNCATING
  EXPECTATION - and the testbench's "expected 199" IS that truncation, so the two
  agree by construction rather than by luck.
  ...
  <b>3. RUN 3 HAS NO TRUNCATION.</b> 8000 = 64 * 125 makes r = 0, so floor is the
  identity and 1375 is the whole product. That is the one input where the word means
  exact with nothing left over - and it is why four targets beat two.
  ...
  A figure that said only "the conversion is exact" was making claim 1 and being read
  as claim 2. Four runs is what makes that impossible to reproduce.
end note

rectangle "THE IMAGE - 387 words of 1024, and that is CORRECT" as IMG <<gate>>
rectangle "firmware/sr04_range.hex\n\n<b>387 words</b>" as H1 <<host>>
rectangle "0xF000 fill\n\n<b>637 words</b>\n\n<b>NOP</b> - never\nexecuted" as H2 <<dev>>
rectangle "halt: JMP halt\n\nthe program never\nreturns here" as H3 <<dev>>

IMG -[#3A7]-> H1
H1 -[#3A7]-> H2
H2 -[#3A7]-> H3

note bottom of H2
  <b>"387 words vs 1024 requested" READS AS A DEFECT AND IS NOT ONE.</b>
  ...
  * the instruction memory is 1 024 words (IMEM_WORDS in the TB, pe_soc.v);
  ...
  * <b>tools/fw/peasm.py pads the image to the requested width with 0xF000</b>, which
    is NOP, and says so in the generated file's own comment;
  ...
  * <b>$readmemh into [0:1023] from a 387-word file leaves 637 words unwritten</b> and
    reports it only in a WARNING, so the testbench now counts the file's own length
    and asks $readmemh for exactly that many words, pre-filling with 16'hF000;
  ...
  * the firmware <b>parks at halt: JMP halt</b>, so the fill is a no-op region behind a
    program that never returns there.
  ...
  The earlier symptom-only narration - an image that looked short - was a consequence
  of the testbench asking $readmemh for more words than the file held. The length is
  counted now, which is why the number is PRINTED rather than inferred from a warning.
end note

note bottom of HDR
  <b>WHY THESE FOUR TARGETS, and it is not "more is better".</b>
  ...
  1 160 us  a small target
  5 816 us  exercises the ten-bit path AND the high byte - an overflow here would be
            invisible from the outside, because the answer would still be a plausible
            millimetre figure
  2 000 us  a mid target
  8 000 us  the r = 0 case, where the conversion's truncation is a no-op
  ...
  <b>THE GAP THAT WAS HERE IS NOW CLOSED, and it is worth recording.</b> The mutation
  suite regress/mutate_timing_tb.sh carries 60 cases across the timing family and
  <b>none are sr04</b>. A suite that no mutant perturbs cannot distinguish "the
  testbench would notice" from "nothing ever tries to break this one". A mutation
  that broke the r = 0 case at 8 000 us, or turned the + 5 back into a + 4, would be
  caught ONLY because a testbench pins 601 as an equality. Routed, not written:
  regress/ is not this branch's surface.
end note

@enduml
